format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-meets-matter

lesson irradiated-or-contaminated
title: Exposed or contaminated
minutes: 6
xp: 50
teaches: u3-exposure-vs-contamination
teaches: u3-exposure-ends
relies_on: u3-ionizing-meaning

description:
    Standing near a source of radiation is not the same as having radioactive material on you. You will find out the difference, and when each one ends.

cite ref-hps-radioactive-contamination

supports:
    Radiation does not get on or in people; radioactive contamination can be spread. A contaminated person is exposed until the radioactive material is removed.

cite ref-who-ionizing-radiation

supports:
    External irradiation stops when the source is shielded or the person moves outside the radiation field; material on skin or clothes can often be washed off; internal exposure stops when the radionuclide leaves the body.

card c01
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: A torch and a pot of glitter

body:
    Shine a torch at someone and the light reaches them. Switch it off, or step out of the beam, and it stops. Now tip a pot of glitter onto their sleeve. The glitter stays, even after they walk away. Radiation can act like the torch. Radioactive material can act like the glitter.

card c02
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Exposed: in the path
figure: exposure-figure
figure_at: 1

body:
    You are exposed when you are in the path of radiation from a source. A medical X-ray is a source outside the body. When the radiation stops reaching you, the exposure is over.

card c03
kind: item
type: item
item: u3-l3-choice-stops
connected: true

card c04
kind: item
